name:Ostende nobis (versus in Rogationibus);
office-part:Versiculus;
mode:;
book:Processionarium O.P. (Cormier), 1913, p. 125 (text);
commentary:Versicle: Art. II § IV 3° (pp. 395-396: "Subscripto modo dicantur Versiculi ad Memorias et ante Laudes, et in Processionibus, et quotiescumque dicitur V. a Sacerdote"): tenor h, the syllables after the last accent of the versicle (marked with the flexa sign in the text) fall to f, as in the example Dignáre me laudáre te, Virgo sacráta. R. Da mihi virtútem contra hostes tuos, alleluia after a half bar as in the example Dícite in natiónibus, allelúia (p. 396). Proparoxytone endings: both syllables after the accent fall (the flexa as defined in Art. III § I, p. 402: geminatur nota re). F clef on the 3rd line as printed.;
transcriber:the tone of the Processionarium O.P. 1913 applied to this text for this site (the book prints no melody with this text);
%%
(f3) <sp>V/</sp>. O(h)stén(h)de(h) no(h)bis,(h) Dó(h)mi(h)ne,(h) mi(h)se(h)ri(h)cór(h)di(h)am(h) tu(h)am,(h) (;) al(h)le(h)lú(h)ia.(f) (::) <sp>R/</sp>. Et(h) sa(h)lu(h)tá(h)re(h) tu(h)um(h) da(h) no(h)bis,(h) (;) al(h)le(h)lú(h)ia.(f) (::)
